    </ol>
  </nav>

  <section id="background">
    <h2>1. Background</h2>

    <div id="deep-background" class="callout">
      <strong>Deep background — explicitly skippable.</strong> If you already know Vea epochs, claim hashes, and the two Arb-origin routes, jump to <a href="#narrow-background">Narrow background</a>.
    </div>

    <h3>Vea’s optimistic bridge model</h3>
    <p>Vea moves a commitment to a batch of messages rather than trusting a single relayer. An <strong>Inbox</strong> on Arbitrum accumulates messages and periodically saves a Merkle-tree state root. An <strong>Outbox</strong> on the destination chain accepts a deposited claim about that root. If nobody challenges, the cheap optimistic path completes. If someone challenges, the canonical Arbitrum bridge carries the true snapshot to settle the dispute.</p>

    <div class="diagram" aria-label="Vea happy and dispute paths">
      <div class="box chain"><b>Arbitrum Inbox</b>Messages accumulate.<br><code>snapshots[E]</code> records a root.</div>
      <div class="arrow">→</div>
      <div class="box chain"><b>Destination Outbox</b>Claimer deposits value and claims the root.</div>
      <div class="arrow">→</div>
      <div class="box right"><b>Happy path</b>No challenge; verification advances.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="diagram" aria-label="Vea dispute path">
      <div class="box chain"><b>Mismatch found</b>Challenger compares the claim with a settled Inbox snapshot.</div>
      <div class="arrow">→</div>
      <div class="box"><b>Canonical message</b><code>sendSnapshot</code> sends the true root from Arbitrum.</div>
      <div class="arrow">→</div>
      <div class="box right"><b>Resolution</b>Outbox identifies the honest party and releases deposits.</div>
    </div>

    <h3>The critical data invariant: the Claim struct is not stored</h3>
    <p>The Outbox stores only <code>claimHashes[epoch]</code>, not the full claim. To challenge, verify, resolve, or withdraw, the bot must reconstruct the exact struct from events and block headers. The contract hashes seven fields:</p>
    <pre><code>hashClaim = keccak256(pack(
  stateRoot,
  claimer,
  timestampClaimed,
  timestampVerification,
  blocknumberVerification,
  honest,          // None, Claimer, or Challenger
  challenger
))</code></pre>
    <p>A single wrong timestamp, address, block number, or <code>honest</code> value changes the hash. State-changing contract calls then reject the struct as an invalid claim. This is why the reconstruction rewrite is safety-relevant, not just an RPC optimization.</p>

    <h3>Epoch timing</h3>
    <p>For period <code>P</code>, epoch <code>E</code> covers timestamps <code>[E×P, (E+1)×P)</code>. The snapshot can still change during that interval. Both Outboxes permit claiming epoch <code>E</code> only during the next epoch, so a claim can occur only in <code>[(E+1)×P, (E+2)×P)</code>.</p>
    <div class="timeline" aria-label="Epoch and claim timing">
      <div class="tick"><b>E×P</b>Epoch E starts<br>snapshot mutable</div>
      <div class="tick"><b>(E+1)×P</b>Epoch E ends<br>claim window opens</div>
      <div class="tick"><b>(E+2)×P</b>Claim window closes</div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout warning"><strong>Finalized is necessary but not sufficient.</strong> A finalized Arbitrum block can still be inside epoch E. Reading <code>snapshots[E]</code> there is immutable but incomplete. A safe decision therefore needs both finality and a timestamp at or after <code>(E+1)×P</code>.</div>

    <h3>Three chains, two routes</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Inbox</th><th>Outbox</th><th>Router / L1 used for Arbitrum finality</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Arb→Eth</td><td>Arbitrum Sepolia (421614)</td><td>Sepolia (11155111)</td><td>Sepolia — same chain as Outbox</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Arb→Gnosis</td><td>Arbitrum Sepolia (421614)</td><td>Chiado (10200)</td><td>Sepolia (11155111) — a different chain</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Block numbers are local coordinates. “Block 8,000,000” on Sepolia says nothing about Chiado block 8,000,000. The Arb→Eth route can hide provider mix-ups because its Outbox and finality chain happen to be the same. Arb→Gnosis exposes them.</p>

    <h3 id="narrow-background">Narrow background: what this task changes</h3>
    <p>The old watcher estimated an epoch’s block, then capped a log query at 1,000 blocks. Other paths queried all historical logs without explicit bounds. Reads that could trigger deposits were not consistently pinned to settled block tags. Startup mainly checked for a private key and discovered many configuration errors later.</p>
    <p>This branch replaces those behaviors with five connected layers:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Validate all static configuration, RPC identity, deployments, and funding before the watch loop.</li>
      <li>Translate protocol timestamps to exact chain-local block numbers.</li>
      <li>Scan logs in bounded, adaptive chunks.</li>
      <li>Reconstruct claims from a complete protocol-derived time window, then verify against the on-chain hash.</li>
      <li>Pin deposit-bearing decisions to finalized blocks after the epoch boundary.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><span class="label inferred">intent: inferred</span> The code comments consistently frame these changes as closing missed-log gaps, surviving provider range limits, and preventing decisions on mutable state. No separate design document states a broader rationale.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="intuition">
    <h2>2. Intuition</h2>

    <h3>Idea A: validate the map before driving</h3>
    <p>Suppose a validator is configured with an Arbitrum URL that actually points to Ethereum mainnet, an empty indexer URL, and a signer with zero gas. Starting the loop and failing one defect at a time is slow and misleading. The new validator first performs cheap static checks, then network checks, collecting all failures before throwing one <code>EnvValidationError</code>.</p>
    <div class="diagram" aria-label="Environment validation phases">
      <div class="box"><b>Static phase</b>Key syntax, lists, supported routes, URL syntax, required token address.</div>
      <div class="arrow">→</div>
      <div class="box"><b>Preflight phase</b><code>eth_chainId</code>, deployed bytecode, native balance, token balance/allowance.</div>
      <div class="arrow">→</div>
      <div class="box right"><b>Watch</b>Emit validated identity and non-fatal funding warnings.</div>
    </div>
    <p>Crucially, the provider used for preflight is not constructed with a trusted chain ID. It asks the endpoint for <code>eth_chainId</code>; otherwise “expected 11155111” could be compared with a provider value already hard-coded to 11155111.</p>

    <h3>Idea B: ask RPCs in pieces, and adapt</h3>
    <p>RPC providers impose different <code>eth_getLogs</code> limits. For a toy range 100–2,500 and chunk size 1,000, the scanner asks:</p>
    <div class="diagram" aria-label="Chunked log scan">
      <div class="box"><b>Chunk 1</b>100…1099</div><div class="arrow">→</div>
      <div class="box"><b>Chunk 2</b>1100…2099</div><div class="arrow">→</div>
      <div class="box"><b>Chunk 3</b>2100…2500</div>
    </div>
    <p>If a provider rejects 1,000 blocks but accepts 500, the same chunk is retried at 500 and that smaller size is retained. Forward scanning with early exit finds the oldest event. Backward scanning with early exit finds the newest event. Results are sorted by block and log index so multiple events in one block remain ordered.</p>

    <h3>Idea C: derive ranges from time on the correct chain</h3>
    <p>Average block time is only an estimate. <code>blockAtTimestamp</code> samples recent blocks, estimates a starting point, expands backward if that point is still too new, and then bisects. Its result is the highest block whose timestamp is not later than the target.</p>
    <pre><code>Toy chain head: block 100,000 at time 2,100,000
Target time:    900,000
Recent estimate may overshoot because older blocks were slower.
Algorithm: estimate → widen until behind target → binary search
Result: exact floor block, never a newer timestamp.</code></pre>
    <p>This matters because an Inbox range must use Arbitrum block numbers, while an Outbox range must use Sepolia or Chiado block numbers. A timestamp is the shared concept; a block number is not.</p>

    <h3>Idea D: reconstruct cheaply, escalate only when needed</h3>
    <p>For epoch 1 with <code>P = 7,200s</code> and a toy 12-second Outbox chain, a claim is possible from blocks near 1,200 through 1,799. The new code adds 256 blocks of padding, scans for the unique <code>Claimed</code> event, and builds the simplest possible struct. If that struct already matches <code>claimHashes[1]</code>, it avoids two more scans. Only a mismatch triggers scans for <code>Challenged</code> and <code>VerificationStarted</code>.</p>
    <div class="diagram" aria-label="Claim reconstruction escalation">
      <div class="box"><b>1. Read together</b>Finalized head + <code>claimHashes[E]</code> at that exact block.</div>
      <div class="arrow">→</div>
      <div class="box"><b>2. Minimal candidate</b><code>Claimed</code> log + block timestamp.</div>
      <div class="arrow">→</div>
      <div class="box"><b>3. Escalate</b>Add challenge/verification fields only if hash mismatches.</div>
      <div class="arrow">→</div>
      <div class="box"><b>4. Fallback</b>Use indexer, but accept only if its struct hashes correctly.</div>
    </div>
    <p>The unknown <code>honest</code> field is recovered by trying None, Claimer, and Challenger. Unlike the old boolean verifier, the new helper returns the matching struct. That distinction is important: callers need the exact preimage for later contract calls.</p>

    <h3>Idea E: pin the verdict, not just the transport</h3>
    <p><code>resolveSettledReadBlocks</code> asks the existing finality checker for finalized L2 and L1 blocks, rejects finality flags, and checks that the finalized Inbox timestamp has crossed the epoch boundary. Claiming and challenging then read snapshots at the returned Inbox block.</p>
    <div class="callout good"><strong>Concrete safety improvement.</strong> The old finality helper could return the latest Arbitrum block in its healthy path. The branch always returns the finalized Arbitrum block and fixes extraction of <code>delaySeconds</code> from <code>maxTimeVariation()</code>; coercing the whole four-value tuple produced <code>NaN</code>.</div>

    <h3>The central failure: one variable, two meanings</h3>
    <p>In both claimer and validator helpers, the code defines:</p>
    <pre><code>const queryRpc = veaRouterProvider ?? veaOutboxProvider;</code></pre>
    <p>That is harmless on Arb→Eth, where router/L1 and Outbox both mean Sepolia. On Arb→Gnosis, <code>queryRpc</code> means Sepolia while <code>veaOutbox</code> means Chiado. The returned “outbox block” is therefore a Sepolia number later used as a Chiado block tag.</p>
    <div class="diagram" aria-label="Cross-chain block number bug">
      <div class="box chain"><b>Arbitrum Sepolia</b>Inbox snapshot<br>block 306,763,304</div>
      <div class="arrow">finality via</div>
      <div class="box wrong"><b>Sepolia</b>Router/L1 finalized<br>block ~8M<br><strong>mislabelled “outboxBlock”</strong></div>
      <div class="arrow">wrong block tag →</div>
      <div class="box wrong"><b>Chiado</b>Actual Outbox<br>expects its own ~10–15M coordinate</div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout danger"><strong>Safety versus liveness.</strong> The supplied review found no wrong-verdict/deposit-loss path from these introduced bugs. Instead, Arb→Gnosis becomes non-operational: claiming silently returns no work, and resolution reads the wrong historical Outbox state or can revert. That is still release-blocking.</div>
  </section>

  <section id="code">
    <h2>3. Code walkthrough</h2>
    <p class="small">Scope verified from Git: 23 files under <code>validator-cli/</code>, 2,444 insertions and 334 deletions. The head branch resolves to <code>67a87fc</code>; the validator changes are in its history, including <code>4772f7b</code>. Claims below come from the scoped diff, surrounding source/contracts, and the supplied independent review.</p>

    <h3>Concern 1 — startup environment validation</h3>
    <p><strong>Files:</strong> <code>utils/envValidation.ts</code> (new), <code>envValidation.test.ts</code> (new), <code>consts/bridgeRoutes.ts</code>, <code>errors.ts</code>, <code>botEvents.ts</code>, <code>logger.ts</code>, <code>watcher.ts</code>, <code>.env.dist</code>.</p>
    <p>The bridge table now carries explicit Inbox and optional Router chain IDs, the environment-variable name for each RPC role, and an optional deposit-token variable. This gives validation enough metadata to say “<code>RPC_GNOSIS</code> should answer as 10200” rather than merely “RPC failed.”</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Validation</th><th>Fatal?</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Private key absent/malformed; empty or unknown chain/network; bad/missing Envio URL; required RPC/token missing</td><td>Yes</td><td>All static problems are accumulated; no network calls occur if any exist.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>RPC unreachable/wrong chain; missing deployed code; zero native gas balance</td><td>Yes</td><td>Preflight collects problems across configured chains, then throws.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Non-zero balance below deposit; WETH balance/allowance below deposit</td><td>No</td><td>Returned as warnings and emitted through <code>ENV_WARNING</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p><code>watch()</code> initializes logging, awaits validation, emits <code>ENV_VALIDATED</code> with only the derived signer address, then parses CLI path/network config and enters the loop. The malformed private-key test asserts the secret itself is never echoed.</p>
    <div class="callout warning"><strong>Deployment migration edge case.</strong> <code>.env.dist</code> changes <code>VEAOUTBOX_CHAINS</code> from <code>11155111,421611</code> to <code>11155111</code>. Old environments retaining dead chain 421611 now fail startup. This matches fail-fast behavior, but the supplied review recommends a migration note.</div>
    <p><span class="label inferred">intent: inferred</span> Fail before appearing healthy and give operators one complete correction list rather than repeated restart/fail cycles.</p>

    <h3>Concern 2 — bounded and adaptive log scanning</h3>
    <p><strong>Files:</strong> <code>utils/logScanner.ts</code> (new), <code>logScanner.test.ts</code> (new), with callers in claim, snapshot, claimer, and Arbitrum finality code.</p>
    <p><code>scanLogs</code> defaults to 1,000-block chunks and halves rejected chunks down to a 32-block floor. It supports forward/backward traversal and optional early exit. <code>findFirstLog</code> and <code>findLatestLog</code> specialize those controls.</p>
    <pre><code>forward + first hit  → oldest matching event
backward + first hit → newest matching event
all returned logs    → ascending (blockNumber, log index)</code></pre>
    <p>The Arbitrum batch lookup also replaces one potentially wide <code>queryFilter</code> with <code>findFirstLog</code>. Tests cover exact tiling, retries, minimum-size failure propagation, traversal direction, same-block index ordering, and empty results.</p>
    <div class="callout good"><strong>Review result.</strong> The backward early-exit algorithm is correct: the first non-empty chunk is the newest chunk containing a match, and sorting makes the final element the newest log in that chunk.</div>

    <h3>Concern 3 — time/block helpers and lookback policy</h3>
    <p><strong>Files:</strong> <code>utils/epochHandler.ts</code> and its tests.</p>
    <p><code>blockAtTimestamp</code> replaces a fixed 500-block average estimate. It adapts to non-uniform block times and uses binary search. <code>getBlockFromEpoch</code> becomes a thin call into it. <code>getLookbackFloorBlock</code> maps “head timestamp minus sequencer delay minus one epoch” to a block on the supplied provider’s chain.</p>
    <p>The seven-day cold-start backlog remains in <code>setEpochRange</code>, which chooses epochs to revisit. It is intentionally absent from “latest event” scans because those have an indexer fallback. <span class="label inferred">intent: inferred</span> Avoid millions of empty Arbitrum log blocks on startup while keeping the broader epoch backlog.</p>
    <div class="callout"><strong>Edge case checked.</strong> If a target predates an explicitly supplied floor, <code>blockAtTimestamp</code> returns the floor even though its timestamp is too new. The review found no current caller that passes such a floor; all production calls use the default zero.</div>
    <div class="callout warning"><strong>Cleanup:</strong> <code>epochHandler.ts</code> contains an author note beginning “DONT DECLARE A NEW var…” and another unresolved prose question above the lookback docstring. These are stale comments, not runtime bugs.</div>

    <h3>Concern 4 — claim reconstruction rewrite</h3>
    <p><strong>Files:</strong> <code>utils/claim.ts</code>, <code>claim.test.ts</code>, and watcher call sites.</p>
    <p>The watcher no longer computes an Outbox epoch block and clips the query to the next 1,000 blocks. Instead, <code>getClaim</code> receives <code>epochPeriod</code> and owns its range derivation:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Fetch the Outbox finalized head.</li>
      <li>Read <code>claimHashes[E]</code> at that exact block number.</li>
      <li>Convert <code>(E+1)P</code> and <code>(E+2)P</code> to Outbox blocks; pad both sides by 256.</li>
      <li>Find the unique <code>Claimed</code> event.</li>
      <li>Build a minimal claim and test its hash.</li>
      <li>If needed, scan from the claim block to the finalized head for challenge and verification events.</li>
      <li>Try all three <code>honest</code> values and return the matching struct.</li>
      <li>If logs fail or do not reconstruct, query the indexer and subject that data to the same hash check.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>This closes the old detection gap in the tail of the claim window and prevents a race where the hash came from a newer head than the scanned logs. Tests explicitly place a claim in the old blind spot, verify full-window coverage, and exercise challenged, verified, resolved, fallback, mismatch, and absent-claim cases.</p>
    <div class="callout good"><strong>Trust boundary preserved.</strong> Indexer data is a discovery aid, not authority: it must produce the Outbox’s on-chain hash before use.</div>

    <h3>Concern 5 — settled-read gating and finality fixes</h3>
    <p><strong>Files:</strong> <code>utils/arbToEthState.ts</code> (plus new tests), <code>helpers/claimer.ts</code>, <code>helpers/validator.ts</code>.</p>
    <p><code>resolveSettledReadBlocks</code> centralizes the “can we decide?” gate. It returns null and emits a diagnostic if the finality checker returns nothing, either finality flag is set, or the finalized Inbox block is before the end of epoch E. The claimer pins <code>snapshots[E]</code>; the challenger pins its verdict snapshot.</p>
    <p>Two lower-level corrections support this:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>maxTimeVariation()</code> is decoded via its named <code>delaySeconds</code> field instead of converting a four-item tuple to a number.</li>
      <li><code>getBlocksAndCheckFinality</code> always returns the finalized Arbitrum block, not the latest tail on a healthy path.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Tests cover undefined finality results, both finality flags, boundary-minus-one rejection, exact-boundary acceptance, and reuse of the already-checked blocks rather than refetching a moving head.</p>

    <h3>Concern 6 — latest snapshot/claim checks</h3>
    <p><strong>Files:</strong> <code>helpers/snapshot.ts</code>, <code>snapshot.test.ts</code>, and watcher wiring.</p>
    <p>The snapshot helper now receives separate Inbox and Outbox providers. It searches each contract on its own chain, backward from finalized head to a protocol-derived lookback floor. If either recent log is absent, it deliberately throws into the existing indexer fallback. This is the correct pattern for the corresponding claimer cold-start case.</p>
    <p><span class="label inferred">intent: inferred</span> Keep routine checks bounded while retaining indexer-backed recovery after downtime.</p>

    <h3>Concern 7 — resolution-state scanning</h3>
